[
  "Welcome to The Sound Above. I am Mike Hall, and this is Episode 1 of our technical rewatch series. Today we are going back to November 19, 2011, on-site at Software Craftsmanship North America at the Swissotel in downtown Chicago. My very first guest when we launched the UGtastic interview series was Sergio Pereira, the organizer of Chicago Alt.NET. There is a reason I asked Sergio to sit down first. Chicago Alt.NET was the very first user group I ever walked into, and the first stage where I ever gave a technical talk.\n",
  "To understand why that room mattered, you have to remember what enterprise .NET felt like in 2007 and 2008. Most corporate shops treated Microsoft's default box products as the law. If a tool or workflow did not ship inside Visual Studio or MSDN, you were told not to touch it. In April 2007, David Laribee published the Alt.NET Manifesto, and that October developers gathered in Austin for the first Alt.NET Open Spaces, which Martin Fowler wrote about in his Bliki. Sergio brought that exact spirit to Chicago. On the second Wednesday of every month at 6:30 PM, right when commuters could still catch the Metra train back to the suburbs, about 25 of us took the elevator up to Redpoint Technologies on the 84th floor of the Sears Tower. On November 12, 2008, I stood up in that room and gave my first talk on NHaml alongside Adam Tybor presenting on the Spark view engine.\n",
  "Here is the question that guides our rewatch today: How do working engineers build practical autonomy inside corporate environments without waiting for vendor permission? Alt.NET was never about hating .NET. As Sergio says right at the start of this tape, we were developers who liked .NET, used it every day, and wanted to bring continuous improvement, automated testing, NHibernate, StructureMap, CodeSharp, and IronRuby into our daily work. When Richard Banks and the community organized the Entity Framework v1 Vote of No Confidence, and Jeremy Miller wrote his March 2008 MSDN Magazine piece on Alt.NET, they proved that practitioners could hold vendor tooling accountable to real engineering standards.\n",
  "Let us roll the 2011 hallway conversation with Sergio Pereira (YouTube ID: qOHdZKz1WFw). Listen to how Sergio describes the charter of the group: not bringing in polished marketing speakers, but encouraging regular team developers to take the talks they gave at lunch with their coworkers and share them with a room of peers who wanted to see a better way to build software.\n",
  "How does this connect to where we sit in 2026? In 2008, the default vendor trap was drag-and-drop designer wizards that hid coupling and made unit testing impossible. Today, the default vendor trap is black-box cloud AI generation that produces thousands of lines of unverified code while hiding the domain boundaries underneath. The lesson from Chicago Alt.NET is unchanged: tools are instruments, not religions. You do not accept a workflow just because a vendor ships it as the default. You keep your domain models clean, you run your own fast local verification loops, and you judge every tool by whether it holds up under test.\n",
  "Thank you for joining Episode 1 of The Sound Above. You can read the full interactive transcript with Sergio Pereira, along with links to David Laribee's 2007 Manifesto, Martin Fowler's Austin notes, and Jeremy Miller's retrospectives, right on the Episode 1 page at just3ws.com. To help support our independent compute and archive preservation, visit patreon.com/whoistechcmty. In Episode 2, we sit down with Ray Hightower of ChicagoRuby to talk about grassroots hospitality and opening doors across communities. Until next time: keep your feedback loops fast, and your code humane.\n"
]